1 NOTE John of England
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=01032142-22b6-401a-bdf9-c2043575be6d&tid=10320707&pid=-600463557
1 NOTE
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  John, King of England, conspired unsuccessfully with Philip, II of France to supplant his older brother, Richard, as King of England when Richard left on the Third Crusade.  On Richard's death, Jo
2 CONC hn ascended to the throne to the exclusion of his nephew Arthur I of Brittany, who, aided by Philip, II of France, began a revolt in France.  John's reign was 1199 - 1216.  Burkes Peerage records 1160
2 CONC  as date of birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Arthur was captured (1202), John lost many of his French possessions to Philip.
2 CONT
2 CONT John objected to Pope Innocent, III's choice for archbishop of Canterbury and the pope placed England under an interdict March 24, 1208 forbidding the clergy to administer sacraments.  John's refusalt
2 CONC o accept the new archbishop led to his excommunication in 1209.  He regained papal favor by surrendering his kingdom to Pope Innocent, III but received it back as a papal fief.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Magna Carta, reaffirming human rights, signed by John at Runnymede in mid-June, 1215 limited the power of the English monarchy.  Feudal barons supported by Scotland's new king Alexander, II met wi
2 CONC th John between Staines and Windsor, exacted major concessions reaffirming traditional feudal privileges contained in the accession charter signed by Henry I a century before and compelled John to set
2 CONC  his seal to the Magna Carta. John immediately appealed to Pope Innocent, III, who issued a bull annulling the charter.  John imported French mercenaries to fight the barons, but the Magna Carta remai
2 CONC ned the basis of English feudal justice.
2 CONT
2 CONT John came down with dysentery in October, 1216 after crushing resistance in the north. He crossed the Wash and reached Newark Castle but died there October 19, 1216 at age 48. The king was succeeded b
2 CONC y his 9-year-old son, John, who reigned until 1272 as Henry, III; the earl of Pembroke William Marshal served as regent, and the moderate party took control, ending the need for opposition to royal au
2 CONC thority. Unknown GEDCOM info: MH:N78 Unknown GEDCOM info: 1309C5BD-3DFC-4D31-AB6F-A9CB4E4D5D7D
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Royal Dau of Engl. p. 158 (GS #13702)
2 CONT     2. Dict. of Nat'l Biog., p. 402-16 (GS #920.042 D561n vol 29)
2 CONT     3. Burke's Peerage 99th Ed., prefix p. 253 (GS #942 D22bup)
2 CONT     4. Also searched without positive results: The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT     Adjusted by Wells F. Collett, 69 South 400 East, Kaysville, Utah
2 CONT     84037, 6 July 1967; Doomsday Book (GS #Q942.51 R2je p. 8);
2 CONT     Burke's Peerage 1883 (GS #942 D33bug p. 196); Derbyshire A
2 CONT     Archaeological and Natural Hist Society (GS #942.51 B2a (1887)
2 CONT     vol 9 p. 119)
2 CONT !HISTORICAL NOTES:
2 CONT     This is King John of the Magna Charta.  The Magna Charta is recognized todayas one of the most important documents in the history of human freedom. Comingnear the end of the Dark Ages, it challeng
2 CONC ed the "divine right" of kings to rulearbitrarily. King John of England was a tyrant who seized the throne in 1199 byimprisoning and probably murdering young Prince Arthur, the rightful heir. KingJohn
2 CONC  threw people into prison at the drop of a hat, confiscated their propertyby heavy taxes and unjust decrees, and despoiled the wives and daughters of hisNobles. His quarrels with the pope caused the p
2 CONC ope to excommunicate all Englandfor a period of six years. The king had a tiresome habit of forcing the peasantsto build bridges over streams he might wish to cross while hunting, and at onetime he ma
2 CONC de the whole of England his own private game preserve, so that none ofthe starving peasantry could kill even a rabbit for supper. The king's Nobles finally refused to fight for him abroad, and as a re
2 CONC sult he was utterly defeatedat the Battle of Bouvines during the summer of 1214. The king then turned in fury on his Barons at home, but he underestimated the powers arrayed against him. The Barons, a
2 CONC ssembled in conference by the Archbishop of Canterbury, each took asolemn oath on the high altar that they would stand united and not relinquish their efforts until they had either compelled the king 
2 CONC to confirm their libertiesor had waged war against him to the death. During the winter of 1214-15, the Barons prepared and presented to King John a list of their demands. When the king saw the Article
2 CONC s, he is supposed to have sworn by "God's teeth", his favorite oath, that he would never agree to such demands or any part of them. But the Baronswere not to be denied. They raised an army and launche
2 CONC d a "holy crusade" against the king to recover their rightful liberties. The king was finally forced to surrender at Runnemede on June 15, 1215. After four days of negotiations, the king's seal was af
2 CONC fixed to all copies of the historic Magna Charta. The Magna Charta is recognized today as a foundation stone of free government and has been referred to as "The Mother of Constitutions". It was an imp
2 CONC ortant forerunner of theUnited States Constitution. Sections 39 and 40 of the Charter state: "No free man shall be taken, or imprisoned, or dispossessed, or outlawed, or banished, or in any way destro
2 CONC yed...except by the legal judgment of his peers or by the law ofthe land. To no one will we sell, to no one will we deny, or delay, right of justice." The Barons offered to their sub-tenants the same 
2 CONC concessions that they were demanding from the king. Section 61 authorized the election of 25 Surety Barons to see that the provisions of the Charter were enforced. These Sureties weregiven the right t
2 CONC o distress and harass the king by any means in their power ifhe did not keep his pledges. Seven of these Sueties are included in the royal line pedigree of this book.
1 NOTE Johan av England (født 24. desember 1166, død 18./19. oktober 1216) var konge av England fra 1199 til 1216. Han arvet tronen etter sin eldre bror kong Richard I (også kjent som Rikard Løvehjerte). Jo
2 CONC han er kjent under klengenavnet ?Johan uten land? og i England kalles han også gjerne ?John Soft-sword?.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fra 1185 hadde han, som den første, tittelen Lord av Irland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kong Johans regjeringstid regnes tradisjonelt som en av de mest katastrofale i engelsk historie. Han startet sin kongsgjerning med å miste Normandie til den franske kongen Filip August, og på slutte
2 CONC n av sin regjeringstid hadde han skapt grobunn for så mye misnøye at England var på full fart inn i borgerkrig, og den tradisjonelt sterke engelske kongemakten ble svekket for alltid ettersom adelen
2 CONC  og kirken tvang kongen til å godta Magna Carta i 1215.
2 CONT
2 CONT Historie
2 CONT Det var tre viktige feil som ble begått av kongen. Den første skyldtes måten han tok makten på, som førte til en generell mangel på respekt. Da Rikard Løvehjerte døde i 1199 var det to kandidater 
2 CONC til tronen: Johan, og hans nevø Arthur av Bretagne, som befant seg i Normandie. Johan tok Arthur til fange, og han ble ikke sett igjen. Det ble aldri bevist at han ble myrdet, men det var den generell
2 CONC e oppfatning at Johan hadde tatt ham av dage, og mange så det som en svart plett på hans rykte at han var villig til å myrde en slektning for å komme til makten.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den andre feilen var at han mislyktes i krigføring i Frankrike. Filip August av Frankrike tok det meste av de engelske besittelsene i landet, og de engelske baronene krevde at Johan måtte ta dem tilb
2 CONC ake. Etter åtte år forsøkte han å gjøre det, men led et avgjørende nederlag i slaget ved Bouvines i 1214.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den tredje store feilen var å bli innblandet i en disputt med kirken om utnevnelsen av ny erkebiskop av Canterbury. Johan ville utnevne en av sine egne menn, mens kirken ville utnevne Stephen Langton
2 CONC . Striden pågikk i flere år, og paven satte hele landet under interdikt. I 1213 måtte kongen gi etter.
2 CONT
2 CONT I populærkulturen
2 CONT Kong Johan er hovedperson i et av William Shakespeares tidlige historiske skuespill, og i Walt Disneys tegnefilm om Robin Hood har han en sentral rolle, da i skikkelsen til en løve.
1 NOTE John married more than once.  One of his queens was only 11 years old at the
2 CONT time.  He was a terrible king. Some of the things that I've read about him make me believe that he will spend eternity in hell.  It seems that he was cruel for the sake of being cruel.  I believe that
2 CONC  he suffered from paranoia.
2 CONT He died from eating improperly preserved peaches and beer.  The resulting
2 CONT condition was dysentary.  His only redeeming quality seems to be that he loved his children.  
